hadiths[4]{hadithnumber,text}:
"394","Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, 'Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Qatadah and Humayd, from al-Hasan, that a man from Quraysh stole a she-camel, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cut off his hand, yet his testimony remained acceptable."
"395","'Abdullah ibn Maslamah narrated to us, 'Abd al-'Aziz — meaning Ibn Muhammad — narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Zayd — meaning Ibn al-Muhajir — from Talhah ibn 'Abdullah — meaning Ibn 'Awf — from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: ""There is no testimony for an adversary nor for one under suspicion."" Abu Dawud said: Al-zanin is the accused, al-jinnah is one in whom there is madness, and al-hinnah is the spiteful."
"396","Al-Qasim ibn 'Isa narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Dhi'b, from al-Hakam ibn Muslim, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-A'raj, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he said: ""The testimony of one under suspicion and rancor and madness is not permissible."" Ibn Abi Dhi'b said: One of suspicion and rancor."
"397","Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us, al-Layth narrated to us, from Bukayr: He heard Ibn al-Musayyib say: Two men disputed before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) over something, and each of them brought upright witnesses of equal number, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cast lots between them and said: ""O Allah, You judge between them."
